      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://www.sciencedaily.com/images/1920/earth-core-structure-layers.webp\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 0.75em;padding:5px 10px;background:#f0f4f8;border-left:3px solid #7b9fc4;border-radius:3px;font-size:0.82em;color:#444;line-height:1.8;\">🌲 <a href=\"https://zirnhelt.github.io/super-rss-feed/review.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.sciencedaily.com%2Freleases%2F2026%2F10%2F261020084816.htm\" style=\"color:#7b9fc4;text-decoration:none;\">✏️</a></p>\nScientists discovered a rock in Papua New Guinea that plunged more than 90 kilometers beneath Earth's surface, revealing a bizarre temperature pattern that challenges established theories of Earth's interior. The unexpected shift from unusually hot to relatively cold conditions could transform our understanding of tectonic plate movements, earthquakes, and volcanoes.",
